WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.265 --> 00:00:01.390
WESLEY CHUN: Hey, students.

00:00:01.390 --> 00:00:03.870
Many of you know how to use
Gmail, Google Drive, and Docs,

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:04.830
and that's great.

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:06.700
But did you can code them too?

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:07.200
Hi.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:09.510
This is Wesley Chun, your
friendly developer advocate

00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:10.710
from Google Cloud.

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:12.390
In this series for
student developers,

00:00:12.390 --> 00:00:15.870
we aim to add G Suite as another
tool in your career portfolio,

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:19.990
giving you the ability to code
those apps you know so well.

00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:21.300
What's G Suite again?

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:24.278
Well, productivity tools like
a word processor, spreadsheets,

00:00:24.278 --> 00:00:26.820
and presentation software in
the form of Google Docs, Sheets,

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:27.570
and Slides.

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:29.790
Add in Gmail, Google
Drive, Calendar,

00:00:29.790 --> 00:00:32.369
and you've got the core
components of G Suite.

00:00:32.369 --> 00:00:35.100
Don't forget about Google
Forms and Hangouts.

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:37.410
Now, paying customers
get even more tools.

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:39.630
While many of you know
how to use G Suite,

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:42.180
behind each app is
a developer API,

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:45.430
meaning you can code Gmail,
Google Drive, Calendar, Docs,

00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:46.930
Sheets, and Slides.

00:00:46.930 --> 00:00:49.800
G Suite is one part of Google
Cloud along with the Google

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:52.560
Cloud Platform, you know,
compute, storage, networking,

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:54.540
security, serverless and
machine learning tools

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:55.860
that you can rent in the cloud?

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:57.277
Well, in addition
to Cloud, Google

00:00:57.277 --> 00:01:00.360
has other apps for users,
like Search, Chrome, YouTube,

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:01.410
and Android.

00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:05.459
But you can't wait for Google
to build every possible app,

00:01:05.459 --> 00:01:07.500
and we don't work
in all industries.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.780
Instead, wouldn't it be
better if we gave you

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:11.880
the tools to build
amazing new apps

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:13.600
to enable your own success?

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:15.960
So you don't have to reinvent
the wheel on the road

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:18.840
to success, we've made a lot
of our tech available to you

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:20.550
in the form of APIs.

00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:23.280
Use them to build your
next web app, mobile app,

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:25.470
or more likely both.

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:28.900
But some have told us that using
Google APIs is challenging.

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:30.450
So let's break
down those barriers

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:32.790
to help everyone
use Google APIs.

00:01:32.790 --> 00:01:36.300
The hub of Google API activity
is the Google Cloud Platform

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:39.090
console that you access at
console.cloud.google.com.

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:43.200
It's where you manage your
apps, called "projects."

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:46.200
Most of its features are
for Google Cloud Platform,

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:50.250
like choosing which GCP tools
to use, managing GCP billing,

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:51.930
managing other
project developers,

00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:53.970
and selecting the
APIs you'll use.

00:01:53.970 --> 00:01:56.950
Only GCP users need
to use the first pair,

00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:59.095
so check out the Cloud
video console to learn more.

00:01:59.095 --> 00:02:01.470
Now, you don't have to worry
about billing in the console

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:05.490
because G Suite and GCP
APIs are built differently.

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:07.330
GCP services are pay per use.

00:02:07.330 --> 00:02:09.330
That's what the billing
component in the console

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:10.199
is for.

00:02:10.199 --> 00:02:13.260
But G Suite API usage is
at no additional charge

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:15.360
on top of its subscription
fee, whether it's

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:18.870
the pay-for Business Edition
or the free Gmail Consumer

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:19.900
version.

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:22.450
Let's take a closer
look at the API Manager.

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:25.920
Now, this developer's
console has three tabs.

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:28.290
The Dashboard is to
see stats on your app,

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:31.110
like how much traffic you're
getting, the number of errors

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:34.480
it's generating, and how
fast it responds to users.

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:38.400
The second tab is to enable
or disable which APIs to use.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:41.320
Now, none of the G Suite
APIs are on by default.

00:02:41.320 --> 00:02:44.280
So pick and choose the
ones to toggle on or off.

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:47.220
The last tab is to create
credentials like API keys

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:48.780
and OAuth clients.

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:50.730
Check out the video
to get more details

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:52.440
on using the dev console.

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:54.630
You can access the
API Manager directly

00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:57.240
at
console.developers.google.com.

00:02:57.240 --> 00:02:59.340
To access Google
APIs, we recommend

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:00.980
using our client libraries.

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:02.760
We'll demo in Python
and JavaScript,

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:04.890
but many other
languages are supported,

00:03:04.890 --> 00:03:07.770
and all of the client libraries
can be downloaded from the link

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:08.970
that you see on screen.

00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:10.470
Before getting
started, heads up,

00:03:10.470 --> 00:03:13.800
there are two types of
APIs, simple and authorized.

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:16.350
Simple APIs are for
accessing public data,

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:18.720
like searching for
places on Google Maps,

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:22.570
querying for YouTube videos, or
sending a sentence to the Cloud

00:03:22.570 --> 00:03:24.670
Natural Language API.

00:03:24.670 --> 00:03:26.550
However, if your
code requires access

00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:31.350
to user or application data,
you need authorized access.

00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:32.370
Make sense?

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:34.840
For user data, that's
user authorization.

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:37.920
And for app data, that's
service account authorization.

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:41.670
Both are basically the same
except for who owns that data.

00:03:41.670 --> 00:03:43.920
Regardless, to get started
with a new project,

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:46.650
go to the API Manager
and create a project

00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:47.940
or reuse an existing one.

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:52.210
Then enable the G Suite or
GCP APIs you wish to use.

00:03:52.210 --> 00:03:54.270
Finally, create the
appropriate credentials

00:03:54.270 --> 00:03:57.000
to talk to Google APIs.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:59.820
Since most G Suite
data is owned by users,

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:02.220
you'll create OAuth 2 client ID.

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:04.650
However, if your data
is owned by an app

00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:07.040
or you're using GCP
services, create

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:09.690
service account OAuth
credentials instead.

00:04:09.690 --> 00:04:11.533
In either case,
download the JSON

00:04:11.533 --> 00:04:13.200
file with those
credentials once they've

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:14.850
been created because
your code needs

00:04:14.850 --> 00:04:17.310
this to talk to Google servers.

00:04:17.310 --> 00:04:19.959
User auth code looks
something like this.

00:04:19.959 --> 00:04:22.538
And by the way, service
account code is similar.

00:04:22.538 --> 00:04:24.330
We're always trying to
improve the library.

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:26.580
So while the names may
change, the structure

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:27.960
will stay consistent.

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:29.370
This example is in Python.

00:04:29.370 --> 00:04:32.490
But regardless of what language
you use, after your imports

00:04:32.490 --> 00:04:34.800
are the permission scopes
that you want your user

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:36.640
to grant to your app.

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:38.490
Next is a security code.

00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:41.250
Now, authorized APIs
require valid OAuth tokens,

00:04:41.250 --> 00:04:43.650
and this block checks
to see if you have them.

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:46.470
If not, it puts together your
credentials plus the permission

00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:48.690
scopes and prompts
the user for it.

00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:51.750
It's that OAuth permissions box
that you see whenever you run

00:04:51.750 --> 00:04:54.150
the app the first time, right?

00:04:54.150 --> 00:04:56.100
Check out the
video for a deeper,

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:58.990
line-by-line
explanation of the code.

00:04:58.990 --> 00:05:00.810
Once you get the
OK from your user,

00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:03.390
create an endpoint
to talk to the API.

00:05:03.390 --> 00:05:05.580
Now service account
auth is a bit simpler

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:08.730
because you don't have to ask
a user to grant permissions.

00:05:08.730 --> 00:05:10.940
The owner of the data,
which could be you,

00:05:10.940 --> 00:05:12.660
will have already
trusted you enough

00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:15.300
to create service account
credentials for you.

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:18.090
Well, whichever you're
using, the auth code

00:05:18.090 --> 00:05:19.975
generally stays the same.

00:05:19.975 --> 00:05:21.600
Now, the only things
that really change

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:24.420
are the bolded lines that you
see, the permissions scopes

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:26.730
and what APIs you use.

00:05:26.730 --> 00:05:29.410
So what does actual
API usage look like?

00:05:29.410 --> 00:05:33.060
Well, everything happens in
a request-response workflow.

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:36.580
Your app makes a request,
credentials are confirmed,

00:05:36.580 --> 00:05:39.670
the request to service
and response is returned.

00:05:39.670 --> 00:05:42.390
It's very much of a
client-server model.

00:05:42.390 --> 00:05:43.230
Congrats.

00:05:43.230 --> 00:05:46.400
You've finished our primer on
Google and especially G Suite

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:47.402
APIs.

00:05:47.402 --> 00:05:48.860
In the next few
videos, we're going

00:05:48.860 --> 00:05:52.350
to dig into actually using
G Suite developer tools.

00:05:52.350 --> 00:05:54.510
If you're still hungry for
more after this series,

00:05:54.510 --> 00:05:56.550
tune in to the G Suite
Dev Show where I help you

00:05:56.550 --> 00:05:57.880
dive even deeper.

00:05:57.880 --> 00:06:00.000
This is Wesley Chun, and
thanks for tuning in.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:03.350
[MUSIC PLAYING]

